David Damerell wrote:
 > Quoting  APLer  <APLer@floor.tilde>:

[lingering on levels as a fighter class]

 >> Non-optimal for sure, but it keeps you alive
 >> until you can get to the mines and gives you the
 >> option to collect both food and armor.

 > No, it doesn't. Monster difficulty _increases with
 > XL_.

Not exactly, it increases with a one-half fraction
of XL, an important difference. That means that
buffing your PC up to XL 30 on DLVL 1 (an absurdity,
but play along with me here) is going to mean that
it is going to be a trip to very deep in the dungeon
indeed before DLVL increases have brought monsters
into the game that are challenging ones to an XL-30
PC. Granted, the monsters will then keep coming at
that difficulty for every level thereafter, by then
the PC is presumably equipped as a walking tank and
traveling with a cadre of fierce pets anyway.

This give the PC _lots_ of time to do time consuming
stuff like pudding farm, altar camp, search every
grid cell for possible traps, dig out every level
finding buried gold or gems, or play extinctionist,
to up the quantity of gathered goodies/valuables and
lower game risk.

 > The Mines are at a fixed depth in a given game;

Not from the viewpoint of they player they aren't,
even though the dungeon layout software sets them
at the start of the game. Until the player actually
finds the depths of all of the Mines entrance, Mines
Town, and Mines End, they are all random values
)constrained within a range) _within the knowledge of
the player_, and the player has play to to optimize
the _player's_ game in which those values are random,
not the dungeon level creation software's game, in
which they are fixed.

 > if you linger on an explored level you are
 > delaying getting to that juicy block of equipment
 > _and_ you are increasing the monster difficulty
 > when you get there.

So what? The monster difficulty increase due to
lingering to buff up XL where monsters are easy is
only half as much as you have increased the PC's
ability to deal with that level of monster
difficulty. That doesn't really sound all that bad
to me.

If you the fast leveling PC are XL 11 in Mines Town
at DLVL 7, you are fighting monsters appropriate to
an evenly leveling XL 9 PC at DLVL9, _in Mines Town
instead_, which certainly does make Mines Town
harder by two monster levels _in the abstract_, but
you then have to look at "harder for whom".

You are also fighting them while being _four_ XLs
higher than the even leveling PC would have been
fighting at XL 7 on DLVL 7, monsters only 2 levels
weaker, which seemingly improves your odds of
success.

The only thing the PC needs to do then at, say,
Mines Town, is to stand at a choke point and kill
off monsters until the ones generated with the level
are exterminated, then cross the level to do hir
business while slaughtering one by one or cluster by
cluster the newly arrived monsters as they are
randomly generated, and doing all this with great
big HPs and experience level associated weapon
bonuses.

=====

And yet you also rage against the opposite approach,
too, that of getting down to Mines Town to buy
protection cheaply because of your low XL, and up to
Sokoban's top level treasure zoo to let your pets
fight foes that are popcorn monsters due to your low
XL, while still at XL 1, in favor of some one
perfect strategy you can't even express coherently
(in major part because it doesn't exist).

You certainly are a piece of work, Damerell.

